# SD Core for Windows documentation
#
# THE MARKDOWN IS THE SOURCE.  The .html and .pdf beside it are generated, and
# the toolchain lives in this repository now (owner's ruling, 26 Aug 2026,
# question 15) so a clone needs nothing else:
#
#   python tools\mkdoc.py --in Testing\markdown --out Testing\html
#   powershell -File tools\mkpdf.ps1 -In Testing\html -Out Testing\pdf
#
# or tools\release.ps1, which renders only what changed and zips the result.
#
# THE LAYOUT.  Three sets, each with the same three folders:
#
#   Testing\    the tester set - what ships with W1.0-0
#   User\       written later
#   Technical\  written later
#
# GENERATED, NOT TRACKED - owner's ruling, 26 Aug 2026, question 16.  The
# pages are rendered after a change, and only the ones that changed.  There
# are eventually two deliverables, and neither is served out of git history:
# a PDF download, and the pages on a web site.  Both are built from the
# Markdown at release time, so tracking a rendered copy would only add a way
# for the two to disagree.
#
# The cost of tracking them is real as well: each PDF is ~200 KB, so a
# one-word fix to a page would commit a quarter-megabyte of unreadable diff.
# And the two directions are not equally reversible - ignored now and tracked
# later costs one deleted line, tracked now and ignored later leaves the
# binaries in history for ever.
*.html
*.pdf

# The release zip tools\release.ps1 writes.  Same reasoning: it is built from
# the Markdown, it is not history.
*.zip

# Windows shell litter
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# Python's byte-cache for tools\.  Generated, machine-specific, and it appeared
# as untracked the first time a tool here was imported rather than run.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
